/* ==============================================================
   07 — Singularities, and passing through them
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   What a singularity is, the three types, and the
             rules for crossing one
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, NO TOOL, generous clearance
   WATCH     The joints, not the tool tip. The whole phenomenon
             is visible in the joints and invisible at the TCP
   TIME      ~90 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   Ϫ READ THIS BEFORE RUNNING

   Crossing a singularity can make joints rotate FAST while the
   tool tip barely moves. That is the defining symptom, and it is
   the hazard: a slow-looking tool tip can mean a wrist snapping
   through 180 degrees.

   This file therefore caps joint speed with SetJointVelLimit
   rather than trusting SetCartLinVel. A Cartesian velocity limit
   does NOT protect you here — it limits the tip, and the tip is
   the thing that is moving slowly.

   Run it with the robot clear of everything, no tool fitted, and
   watch it the first time before you change any numbers.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   WHAT A SINGULARITY IS

   Not a maths problem. A physical blockage.

   Put the robot at its zero position — all six joints at zero —
   and try to move the tool sideways, parallel to the Y axis of
   the base. You cannot. Not "the controller refuses"; the arm
   physically cannot generate motion in that direction from that
   posture. To move that way it must first rotate joints 4 and 6
   a quarter turn in opposite directions.

   That is a singularity: a posture where the end-effector loses
   the ability to move in some direction even though no joint is
   at its limit. It is a kind of workspace boundary.

   Mathematically, it is where several joint-set solutions for a
   pose collapse into one, or where there are infinitely many.
   Which is the same thing said in a less useful way.

   WHY THE MECA500 IS EASY TO REASON ABOUT HERE

   The axes of the last three joints all intersect at one point —
   the wrist centre. That makes all three singularity types
   describable geometrically, so you can tell by eye whether you
   are near one. On many other designs you cannot.

   THE THREE TYPES

   1. WRIST singularity — theta5 = 0.
      Joints 4 and 6 become collinear, so they can rotate in
      opposite directions at equal speed with the end-effector
      completely stationary. The most common one by far; the
      manual says plainly that you will run into it frequently.

      Note what this means: THE ZERO POSITION IS A WRIST
      SINGULARITY. Every example in this repo starts and ends at
      one. You have been visiting singularities all along.

   2. ELBOW singularity — the arm fully stretched, so the wrist
      centre lies in the plane of the axes of joints 2 and 3.
      On the Meca500 this is exactly

          theta3 = -arctan(60/19) = -72.43 degrees

      independent of the other joints. You meet it reaching for
      poses too far from the base.

   3. SHOULDER singularity — the wrist centre lies on the axis of
      joint 1. You meet it working too close to that axis, i.e.
      directly above the robot's own base. Rarest of the three
      and much the most awkward.

   These three correspond exactly to the conditions under which
   the posture parameters cs, ce and cw become undefined. See
   intermediate/04_posture_configuration.mxprog for those.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE RULES FOR CROSSING

   This is the part worth memorising, because the behaviour
   differs per command and per singularity type.

     MoveJoints   Always fine. You name joint angles, so no
                  inverse kinematics is solved and there is
                  nothing to be singular. Joint moves can sail
                  straight through any singularity.

     MovePose     Fine. The manual is explicit: the robot can
                  transition through, or begin or end at, singular
                  postures without any issues.

     MoveLin*     The interesting case. Since firmware 9.1 it can
                  START AT or PASS THROUGH wrist and shoulder
                  singularities, and can END AT any of the three.
                  Crossing respects your posture configuration
                  settings — which is what the demos below are
                  about.

   Two asymmetries worth noting:

   - ELBOW singularities are not in the pass-through list. You
     can end at one. Crossing one mid-path with MoveLin is a
     different matter, and the manual does not promise it.

   - SHOULDER singularities can only be crossed with
     SetAutoConf(1). The robot will never stop at one to reorient
     joints 1, 4 and 6 with the tool stationary, so a demanded
     configuration cannot be honoured through one.

   AND THE WARNING THAT MATTERS MOST

   Passing EXACTLY through a singularity works. Passing NEARLY
   through one is where applications go wrong. Shift the same
   path by one micrometre and joints 4 and 6 — or 1, 4 and 6 —
   rotate very fast while the tool speed collapses.

   So the manual's guidance, and this file's: crossing exists to
   make programming easier, and singularities should be avoided
   in production. Passing through or near them means longer cycle
   times. Use this file to understand the phenomenon so you can
   design paths that stay away from it.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


/* --- Safety settings for this file ---------------------------- */

// The important one. Caps how fast any joint may turn, for EVERY
// Move* command. This is what protects you when a wrist flips.
SetJointVelLimit(10)

SetJointVel(15)
SetCartLinVel(30)
SetBlending(0)          // see each arrival distinctly


/* ============================================================
   DEMO 1 — the blocked direction
   ============================================================
   Go to the zero position, which is a wrist singularity, and
   feel the blockage described at the top of this file. */

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)

/* This MoveLin asks for 50 mm of pure Y from the singular
   posture — the direction that is blocked at the instant it
   starts. Watch the wrist: the motion is only possible because
   joints 4 and 6 reorient first. Starting a linear move AT a
   wrist singularity is explicitly supported, so this is accepted
   rather than refused.

   Worth doing by hand as well, if you have the robot in front of
   you: stop here, jog in base-frame Y from the zero position in
   the MecaPortal, and watch what joints 4 and 6 have to do to
   deliver a sideways millimetre. */
MoveLin(190, 50, 308.9, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* ============================================================
   DEMO 2 — crossing a wrist singularity, AutoConf
   ============================================================
   The robot passes through the all-zeros posture mid-path and
   comes out the other side with its wrist configuration flipped.

   With automatic posture selection on, the controller is free to
   change cw from 1 to -1 as it crosses, and the move succeeds as
   one continuous MoveLin. */

SetAutoConf(1)
SetAutoConfTurn(1)

// Approach the start of the line.
MovePose(190, -60, 308.9, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)

/* One straight line, right through the singular point at
   y = 0. Watch joint 5 pass through zero, and watch joints 4
   and 6 do the work of getting the wrist to the other side. */
MoveLin(190, 60, 308.9, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1.5)


/* ============================================================
   DEMO 3 — the same move, refused
   ============================================================
   Now demand a specific posture instead of letting the robot
   choose, and ask for exactly the same line.

   The robot refuses. Crossing the singular point while holding
   cw = 1 would require joint 4 to rotate 180 degrees at the
   crossing, and joint 4's range is +/-170. It cannot, so it
   declines the whole move rather than starting one it cannot
   finish.

   This is the lesson of the demo: the capability to cross is
   real, but it is the AUTOMATIC selection that buys it. Pin the
   configuration and you give it up. */

MovePose(190, -60, 308.9, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)

SetConf(1, 1, 1)        // also switches automatic selection off

// Expect a refusal and an error here. Nothing should move.
MoveLin(190, 60, 308.9, 0, 90, 0)

/* Reset the error before continuing: press the run button (now
   red), then resume. Or send ResetError() and ResumeMotion()
   from the editor. */
Delay(1)


/* ============================================================
   DEMO 4 — stationary reorientation at the singularity
   ============================================================
   A third behaviour, and the strangest to watch.

   There is a second singular joint set with the same TCP pose as
   all-zeros:

       theta1 = theta2 = theta3 = theta5 = 0
       theta4 = 90, theta6 = -90

   Same tool position, same tool orientation, different joint
   set. That is what a singularity is: solutions collapsing.

   Starting from a posture on that branch and demanding
   cw = -1, the robot WILL execute a MoveLin through it — but on
   arriving at the singular point, joint 4 rotates -180 degrees
   and joint 6 rotates +180 degrees SIMULTANEOUSLY, while the
   tool stays completely still. Then it carries on along its
   line.

   Watch the arm, not the tip. The tip pauses; the wrist spins.
   Nothing is wrong — the robot is reconfiguring in place because
   that is the only way to honour the configuration you demanded
   and still finish the line. */

SetAutoConf(1)

// Land exactly on that second singular joint set.
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 90, 0, -90)
Delay(1.5)

// Out to the start of the line, on this branch.
MovePose(190, -60, 308.9, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)

// Through it. Expect the stationary 180-degree wrist swap.
MoveLin(190, 60, 308.9, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1.5)

SetAutoConf(1)


/* ============================================================
   DEMO 5 — the elbow singularity
   ============================================================
   Purely a theta3 condition, so it is the easiest of the three
   to hit deliberately and the easiest to recognise: the arm is
   straight.

   theta5 is set to 45 here so the posture is elbow-singular
   WITHOUT also being wrist-singular. At theta5 = 0 you would be
   sitting on two singularities at once, which muddles the
   lesson. */

MoveJoints(0, 0, -72.43, 0, 45, 0)
Delay(2)

/* From here, try to move the tool further away from the base
   along a straight line and it cannot — the arm is already
   fully stretched. This is the singularity you meet by asking
   for poses that are simply too far out.

   Note that you CAN end a MoveLin here, which is what matters in
   practice: reaching a far pose is fine, it is trying to
   continue past it that is not. */

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* ============================================================
   DEMO 6 — the shoulder singularity
   ============================================================
   The awkward one. It happens when the WRIST CENTRE sits on the
   axis of joint 1 — directly above the base.

   The difficulty is that the wrist centre is not a place you
   normally have coordinates for. The manual gives a neat trick:
   move the TCP to the wrist centre, and then the singularity is
   simply "x = 0 and y = 0".

   The wrist centre is 70 mm back along the flange axis, hence
   the negative Z. */

SetTrf(0, 0, -70, 0, 0, 0)      // TCP now AT the wrist centre
SetWrf(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)        // measure from the robot base

/* With those two frames set, any pose with x = 0 and y = 0 is a
   shoulder singularity. Approach one, then cross it.

   Remember: shoulder singularities can ONLY be crossed with
   automatic posture selection on. The robot will never stop at
   one to reorient joints 1, 4 and 6 in place the way it did at
   the wrist in demo 4 — so there is no SetConf equivalent of
   that behaviour here. Crossing also necessarily flips cs. */

SetAutoConf(1)

MovePose(0, -60, 300, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)

// Straight across the axis of joint 1. Watch joint 1: it has to
// swing through a half turn as the wrist centre crosses over.
MoveLin(0, 60, 300, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1.5)


/* ============================================================
   DEMO 7 — why "nearly" is worse than "exactly"
   ============================================================
   The most practically important demo in this file.

   Repeat demo 6's crossing, but 0.5 mm off the axis instead of
   on it. Exactly on the singularity, the crossing is clean.
   Just beside it, the joints have to race around to keep the tip
   on a straight line, and the tip slows right down to let them.

   Same command, same speed setting, same 120 mm of travel —
   dramatically worse motion. This is what "avoid singularities
   in production" actually means, and it is why a path that nearly
   touches one is a cycle-time problem, not just an aesthetic
   one. */

MovePose(0.5, -60, 300, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1)

MoveLin(0.5, 60, 300, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(1.5)


/* --- Put everything back ------------------------------------- */

SetTrf(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
SetWrf(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
SetAutoConf(1)
SetAutoConfTurn(1)
SetJointVelLimit(100)
SetBlending(100)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   ® A NOTE ON THE COORDINATES IN THIS FILE

   The singular JOINT SETS here come straight from the manual and
   are exact: all-zeros and {0,0,0,90,0,-90} for the wrist,
   theta3 = -72.43 for the elbow, and the TCP-at-wrist-centre
   trick for the shoulder.

   The Cartesian start and end poses of the MoveLin demos are
   constructed — lines through the singular point, built to match
   the behaviour the manual describes rather than copied from it.
   They should behave as annotated, but the exact acceptance or
   refusal of a given line depends on your TRF and on where the
   robot starts. If a demo behaves differently from its comment,
   trust the robot and tell us: open an issue.

   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Demo 2 then demo 3, back to back. Identical MoveLin, one
      succeeds and one is refused, and the only difference is
      SetAutoConf(1) versus SetConf(1,1,1). This is the clearest
      way to show what automatic posture selection is actually
      for.

   2. Demo 4 is the one people remember. The tool tip stops dead
      while the wrist spins through half a turn. Cover the base
      and ask someone to explain what they just saw.

   3. Demo 6 then demo 7. Change the 0.5 to 5, then to 0.05, and
      watch the motion get worse as you get closer without
      touching. The penalty is not linear and it is not small.

   4. Set SetJointVelLimit(100) at the top and re-run demo 7 — at
      your own risk, with the robot clear. The joint speeds that
      a near-singular path demands are the whole safety argument
      for this file, and they are startling.

   5. Put the robot at all-zeros and run SetAutoConf(0). The
      controller has to pick a configuration and cannot, because
      the posture is singular — so it chooses a boundary one,
      {1,1,1}. A small thing, but it shows that "the current
      configuration" is not always a well-defined idea.

   NEXT   Nothing — this is the last file. Back to the README,
          and to DISCLAIMER.md for the list of places this repo
          simplifies, including this topic.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
